+# The Daily Grind
+
+Static coffee homepage with a browser-encrypted delivery-note workspace.
+
+## Build and checks
+
+- `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`
+- `pnpm build` bundles the browser module into `assets/delivery-notes.js`.
+- `pnpm test` checks all encryption modes, tamper rejection, wrong recipient keys, randomness, and the older-browser P-256 fallback.
+
+The committed browser bundle lets the existing static host serve the homepage without a build step. Rebuild it after modifying `src/`. Encryption requires HTTPS or localhost and Web Crypto support.
+
+## Encryption
+
+The default is noble's `ml_kem768_x25519` hybrid KEM, combining ML-KEM-768 with X25519. Standalone ML-KEM-768 is also available. Classical mode uses native X25519 key agreement, falling back to native P-256 only when X25519 is unsupported. Post-quantum modes are bundled locally; they do not silently downgrade or fetch cryptographic code from a CDN.
+
+Each save creates a recipient key pair and a fresh encapsulation or ephemeral sender key pair. The sender function receives only the public key. HKDF-SHA-256 with a random 32-byte salt and a protocol-specific context derives a nonextractable AES-256-GCM key. Every encryption uses a fresh random 12-byte IV and a 128-bit authentication tag. Protocol version, record ID, recipient fingerprint, suite, cipher, capsule, salt, and IV are bound through authenticated additional data. The downloaded JSON contains the ciphertext and public envelope fields, never the private recipient key or plaintext.
+
+## Integration boundary
+
+This repository has no order API or provisioned delivery-recipient public key. The UI therefore clearly presents a local demo: notes, ciphertext, and recipient private keys remain in this tab's memory and are lost on reload. A downloaded envelope cannot be recovered after the tab is closed. Nothing is submitted to a courier or persisted in browser storage. The preview can be unlocked locally; it automatically hides after 30 seconds or when the page is hidden. Replacing or deleting a note drops the old key, with best-effort zeroing for library-managed private key bytes. JavaScript strings and garbage-collected memory cannot guarantee secure erasure.
+
+Before integrating real delivery, provision an authenticated public key from the recipient, keep its private key exclusively with that recipient, bind the envelope to real order/recipient identifiers, and submit only the envelope to the order API. The local unlock action models recipient decryption; it is not account authentication or access control. Client encryption does not protect against malicious scripts executing in the same page.
